6. A derivation habit
When an identity is unfamiliar, rebuild it in this order:

Name the candidate set and the desired subset.
State what one term, or one coefficient, counts.
Explain why choices multiply, or why cases add.
Test at \(n = 0, 1, 2\) or on a board you can enumerate by hand.
Only then choose a DP, a convolution, or a generating function.

The errors that cost contests are conceptual, not syntactic:

confusing \(k!\left\{ {n \atop k} \right\}\) (named groups) with \(\left\{ {n \atop k} \right\}\) (unnamed groups);
dividing by \(k!\) when it is zero in the working characteristic;
using IE when the intersections are harder than the original condition;
applying the rook product rule to board pieces that share a row or column;
counting rotations or reflections as distinct when the problem treats them as identical — that is Chapter 05.

Prerequisites and learning objectives
You need arithmetic with integers and fractions, and nothing else. The notation reference collects conventions used throughout the book.
By the end of this chapter you should be able to:

read a quantified statement (“for every … there exists …”) and say precisely what a counterexample to it would look like;
pick a proof technique — direct, contrapositive, contradiction, induction — for a claim, and know why each one is valid;
state an invariant for a process and use it to prove that a target state is unreachable, or a potential function to prove that a loop ends;
derive Bézout’s identity from Euclid’s algorithm and use it to decide when \(ax + by = c\) has integer solutions and what all of them are;
justify a counting formula by exhibiting a bijection or by counting one set in two ways.

If all of this is familiar, skim the section headings and jump to the exercises; they are designed to locate gaps rather than to gatekeep.

Suppose you must count the pairs \((x,y)\) with \(1 \le x, y \le 10^7\) and \(\gcd(x,y) = 1\). Iterating over pairs is \(10^{14}\) operations. Iterating over gcd values is \(10^7\). Same problem, different index, seven orders of magnitude.
That is the whole subject of this chapter. Nothing here is about numbers being mysterious; everything is about re-indexing a sum so the work collapses:

from integers to their prime powers, because most quantities of interest are determined independently at each prime;
from pairs to their gcd, turning an exact condition into a weighted sum over divisors;
from all \(n\) values of \(\lfloor n/i \rfloor\) to the \(O(\sqrt n)\) distinct ones, because the quotient is constant on long stretches.

Learn the three re-indexings and the formulas write themselves. Memorize the formulas instead and you will be stuck the moment a problem perturbs them.

